## Proof

We determine the maximum possible cardinality of an infinite set $S$ of positive real numbers such that every non-empty finite subset of $S$ has sum at most $1$.

**Claim.** Such a set $S$ is at most countably infinite, and countably infinite is achievable. Hence the cardinality is $\aleph_0$.

### Upper bound: $S$ is countable

For each integer $n \geq 1$, define
$$A_n = \left\{s \in S : s > \tfrac{1}{n}\right\}.$$

Suppose for contradiction that $|A_n| \geq n+1$ for some $n$. Then we may select $n+1$ distinct elements $s_1, \dots, s_{n+1} \in A_n$. Since each $s_i > 1/n$,
$$\sum_{i=1}^{n+1} s_i > \frac{n+1}{n} = 1 + \frac{1}{n} > 1,$$
contradicting the hypothesis that every finite subset of $S$ sums to at most $1$. Therefore $|A_n| \leq n$, so each $A_n$ is finite.

Since every element of $S$ is a positive real, every $s \in S$ satisfies $s > 1/n$ for all sufficiently large $n$. Consequently
$$S = \bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n.$$

This expresses $S$ as a countable union of finite sets, so $S$ is at most countable.

### Lower bound: countably infinite is achievable

Let
$$S = \left\{2^{-k} : k = 1, 2, 3, \dots\right\} = \left\{\tfrac12, \tfrac14, \tfrac18, \dots\right\}.$$

This is countably infinite. For any finite subset $\{2^{-k_1}, \dots, 2^{-k_m}\}$ with distinct $k_i$,
$$\sum_{i=1}^{m} 2^{-k_i} \leq \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} 2^{-k} = 1.$$

Thus $S$ satisfies the condition and has cardinality $\aleph_0$.

### Conclusion

Any such infinite set $S$ is countable, and a countably infinite example exists. Therefore the cardinality of $S$ is

$$\boxed{\aleph_0}.$$

